#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216929 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "54d19544-7156-58e9-a090-12f8e9492226" :knowledge-point/title "Testing Integrating Factors for ODEs in Differential Form"}] :question/id #uuid "b32d0028-8e5c-5f8a-b989-a7ceff5c1fc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-20896" :question/problem "Show that $I(x) = \\dfrac{1}{xy^3}$ is an integrating factor of the equation \n\n$$\n(x^2y-y^3) \\,\\textrm{d}x - x^{3} \\,\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y] = 0\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x{y}^{3}}(({x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y^2}-\\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad Q(x,y) =- \\dfrac{x^2}{y^3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x})=-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}})=-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{is}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x)=\\dfrac{1}{xy^3}$ $\\text{is}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216930 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a016197-61af-57f3-9c3a-05410db1374d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inexact Differential Equations Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "106cd4dd-2f3f-541e-899a-e2776a39e382" :question/math-academy-id "e-20898" :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n\\left(x + y^2\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}x + \\left(xy + \\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=x+y^2,\\qquad Q(x,y)=xy+\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation." :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int {x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x[(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{({x}^{2}y+2)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{P}(x,y)=x^2+xy^2,\\qquad\n\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)=x^2y+2.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\widetilde{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}y+2)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+2y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\qquad G(x)=\\frac{x^3}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^3}{3}+\\frac{x^2y^2}{2}+2y=c.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216931 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cbedeed-6ac6-5ed8-bd25-9a85eda9577b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integrating Factor for an Inexact Equation: Condition on X Satisfied"}] :question/id #uuid "b69fa09b-2661-5edc-8fcb-c29120e56325" :question/math-academy-id "e-20935" :question/problem "Find an integrating factor for the following equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nx\\,y' = 4y - 3x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q} \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right) = g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x) = \\exp\\left({\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form: \n\n$$\n(3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x  + (x) \\;\\textrm{d} y  =0\n$$\n\nLet's denote \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 3x - 4y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x-4y)=-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{x}(-4-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -\\frac{5}{x}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|) \\\\  & ={x}^{-5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(x) \\cdot \\left((3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + x\\;\\textrm{d} y\\right) =  I(x)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx^{-5} \\cdot \\left((3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + x\\;\\textrm{d} y\\right) = 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216952 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "161b35e2-52bf-5f27-9fc3-bfe34eeb7935" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integrating Factor for an Inexact Equation: Condition on Y Satisfied"}] :question/id #uuid "294fc38f-1d72-52ed-a11d-d1003fb1b1bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-21772" :question/problem "Find an integrating factor for the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy \\; \\textrm{d}y = 0, \\qquad y > 0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{P} \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right) = h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y) = \\exp\\left({-\\int h(y)\\,\\textrm d y}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(y)$ denotes the exponential function $e^y.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =  y^2, \\qquad Q(x,y) =  xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}(2y-y) \\\\  & ={y}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -({y}^{-1})\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & ={y}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot \\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy\\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = I(y)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y^{-1}\\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy \\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = 0}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a016197-61af-57f3-9c3a-05410db1374d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inexact Differential Equations Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e958fa7a-2530-5e28-9195-e3dd1010f6b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216568} {:answer/id #uuid "0c1ef443-4597-541d-a8b8-ab8eac9b4807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216569} {:answer/id #uuid "331ca236-b1f1-5b54-9bf6-4ef67cedef79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216570} {:answer/id #uuid "64939ccc-a9bb-5407-afbc-0c31373b00bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c1ef443-4597-541d-a8b8-ab8eac9b4807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216569} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b6ed42b-5c85-5ddb-bee9-efa742312304" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216567} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f23ef2ff-c615-5327-8856-dcaefed546c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2}y^{4} + 3x^{2}y^{2} + xy = c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216573} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9098f4-a6d7-59b2-aa95-8e2743fc9f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{4}y^{3} + 2x^{3}y^{2} + y = c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216574} {:answer/id #uuid "139036de-8f75-5274-bd83-8a15afa69a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}y^{3} + 2xy^{2} + y = c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216575} {:answer/id #uuid "1ffffdcd-1009-542a-b618-7670fe4f7c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}y^{2} + xy^{2} + xy = c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff9098f4-a6d7-59b2-aa95-8e2743fc9f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{4}y^{3} + 2x^{3}y^{2} + y = c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216574} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ccc11c6-641b-5db3-a990-fccf006a1b53" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1effee9c-dd60-525b-b725-817b354982d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-337096" :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n(\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{-1}\\text{ }\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}[(\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{(6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{(4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y=c.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "54d19544-7156-58e9-a090-12f8e9492226" :knowledge-point/title "Testing Integrating Factors for ODEs in Differential Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036df0a1-b413-53c0-bbe7-5bbb1a2c4f11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344494} {:answer/id #uuid "39a01bc4-272f-55a9-a225-0ba20fa33fe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344495} {:answer/id #uuid "d04c5f69-1f22-5fc5-ade4-8af78146fe3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344496} {:answer/id #uuid "1f3eb193-4caf-54cc-a47c-4479634e88aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f3eb193-4caf-54cc-a47c-4479634e88aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344497} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e3c39b7-fecf-5ea0-8367-f34a03bcccc8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344493} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6446e92-5f38-57da-a4bd-00eb05bb12db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344499} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d4a7af-7fa2-5bd2-9871-68f48fcb2274" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344500} {:answer/id #uuid "4a49eafd-dd4d-531d-951a-94745699c6f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344501} {:answer/id #uuid "7673a0c2-f541-5106-b170-eca486604d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7673a0c2-f541-5106-b170-eca486604d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344502} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ad9b733-f0e8-5f54-9ec2-875b20d6776a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344498} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e36e8e1-969a-5fc8-8093-affe26cf4eea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344504} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e0d551-98c6-530e-8005-73b347f44181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344505} {:answer/id #uuid "ad44e279-135a-5634-93f8-e1602a3052cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344506} {:answer/id #uuid "19f793d9-2009-5954-aeb6-05431dd72062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19f793d9-2009-5954-aeb6-05431dd72062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344507} :answer-field/id #uuid "52501adb-8fc1-5c2b-a60d-4741563c19b1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344503} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74e26ef8-2313-52ad-9aef-bf9fee2342d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344509} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c29c4a-68cd-5468-8ca2-19ac945c0392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74e26ef8-2313-52ad-9aef-bf9fee2342d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344509} :answer-field/id #uuid "61e7cb3a-4c79-5ba7-9efe-17ed5340f008" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344508} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a918c48d-9d98-55ba-af3c-b9ba9c29b085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344512} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0c2c03-08f8-559e-b971-7246ae4d6f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb0c2c03-08f8-559e-b971-7246ae4d6f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344513} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dd45f32-d837-5453-ae84-5c7c1563bb56" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344511} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95a280bd-178f-58d1-a92f-991de55ddd28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344515} {:answer/id #uuid "52b71f51-8ff7-5427-aeb7-96eeecb33952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52b71f51-8ff7-5427-aeb7-96eeecb33952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344516} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf13c10b-f5e0-504f-a747-5f9ce7e7a721" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9a8802d-3d4d-5564-8390-a80c9704bb8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337133" :question/problem "Consider the following inexact differential equation.\n\n$$\nx\\,\\text{d}y-y\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo show that $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}$ is an integrating factor for the given differential equation, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=0\n$$\n\nFor this equation, let's denote\n\n$$\nP=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}},\\,Q=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{{field-4}},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation {{field-5}} exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}$ {{field-6}} an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y]=0\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}(x\\,\\text{d}y-y\\,\\text{d}x) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})=-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{is}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{3}}$ $\\text{is}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cbedeed-6ac6-5ed8-bd25-9a85eda9577b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integrating Factor for an Inexact Equation: Condition on X Satisfied"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "309ee086-ad04-5a47-85c1-faa9cfc0cfa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344519} {:answer/id #uuid "2dcc2951-2805-54af-b64a-61199ad49ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344520} {:answer/id #uuid "9baf72ee-2e6c-53b5-be3c-9512d92b5d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344521} {:answer/id #uuid "e40c84a2-1265-5fa6-a020-ac095520b765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dcc2951-2805-54af-b64a-61199ad49ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344520} :answer-field/id #uuid "388368d0-086a-5c67-b7e8-993b26f4f89e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344518} {:answ