{∂Q}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4582d2d2-2d62-5f11-ac28-54d2c0f0516b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344576} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca938e38-e87a-57fa-a283-fb677ce6d04e" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344574} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2502487b-dec9-5313-8445-df8985d060cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344578} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ed5b92-804b-56df-badb-b2f04f26f0c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2502487b-dec9-5313-8445-df8985d060cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344578} :answer-field/id #uuid "13731a20-c805-5b05-aef4-16d84a3ffdf8" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344577} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3be4ee8-54da-5d56-93b5-5bddb2ba8cbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344581} {:answer/id #uuid "293f8e4c-6d11-59cd-9233-1357cd93e80f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3be4ee8-54da-5d56-93b5-5bddb2ba8cbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344581} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b0ce49c-8c62-5140-bb65-344f5053abbf" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31218718-9382-5519-9081-5b3246a3f4d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-337259" :question/problem "Consider the following inexact differential equation.\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo show that $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ is an integrating factor for the given differential equation, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=0\n$$\n\nFor this equation, let's denote\n\n$$\nP=-y,\\,Q=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}.\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{{field-4}},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation {{field-5}} exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ {{field-6}} an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y]=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}({x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x) & =0 \\\\ -y\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-y,\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})=\\frac{2x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{isn't}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ $\\text{isn't}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation."}