db/ref 17592186344620} {:answer/id #uuid "c486e495-c525-5e31-811b-6d42576cb4cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{-1}((4{x}^{2}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x+3x\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344621} {:answer/id #uuid "05c3eed3-5cf4-524c-a200-e752a84e973f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x((4{x}^{2}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x+3x\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344622} {:answer/id #uuid "add7f7df-fff6-5be0-a745-5bb3eb5123cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}((4{x}^{2}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x+3x\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05c3eed3-5cf4-524c-a200-e752a84e973f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x((4{x}^{2}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x+3x\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344622} :answer-field/id #uuid "6adf24e6-4d1c-5b15-a705-733176bd9921" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89937b0f-baea-5172-ac5f-17e646ff4d1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-337523" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n3x\\,{y}^{′}=-6y-4{x}^{2}\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form. This gives\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+6y,\\,Q(x,y)=3x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $x$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-7}},\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form: \n\n$$\n(4{x}^{2}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x+3x\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nLet's denote \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+6y,\\,Q(x,y)=3x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}+6y)=6 \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}3x=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{3x}(6-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(x)\\cdot ((4{x}^{2}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x+3x\\,\\text{d}y)=I(x)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx\\cdot ((4{x}^{2}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x+3x\\,\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "161b35e2-52bf-5f27-9fc3-bfe34eeb7935" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integrating Factor for an Inexact Eq