d #uuid "bc623b07-fdb7-5aef-b834-e70a6b70d9ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344653} :answer-field/id #uuid "c16ef490-2bbf-52df-b0d5-5737a5457101" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344650} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72b9f9c5-2e07-5d97-8e23-bb1b9bf6d8e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}\\cdot (2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344655} {:answer/id #uuid "79bef994-19aa-530a-b936-c850a96b87d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{-2}{e}^{1/(2y)}\\cdot (2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344656} {:answer/id #uuid "b47cb404-1f29-5aa1-99bd-feac66346c54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{-3}{e}^{-1/(2y)}\\cdot (2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72b9f9c5-2e07-5d97-8e23-bb1b9bf6d8e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}\\cdot (2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344655} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb1ccea1-086c-5320-b04a-c69042c4e25b" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2468ae6a-5d7a-552b-a271-4a092bb2d9ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-337619" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y=0,\\,y>0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=-xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-5}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $y$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y).\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=-xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{3})=6{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-xy)=-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{3}}(6{y}^{2}-(-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{y}^{3}}(6{y}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{6y+1}{2{y}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -\\frac{6y+1}{2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{1}{2y})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\cdot \\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\frac{1}{2y}) \\\\  & ={y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot (2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}{e}^{1/(2y)}(2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a016197-61af-57f3-9c3a-05410db1374d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inexact Differential Equations Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f81643fb-31f9-5735-8eea-48718cca96eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344660} {:answer/id #uuid "b343c865-e53f-5df8-a2dc-7d8b35613d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344661} {:answer/id #uuid "6b48dcfa-c5cb-50d7-8901-6b233e81328c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344662} {:answer/id #uuid "e54180f2-51cd-5305-809d-1807e5871b82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e54180f2-51cd-5305-809d-1807e5871b82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344663} :answer-field/id #uuid "55c1fbdb-01e9-594d-a2e9-b08eba986409" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344659} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa1d0c2a-f56e-5eae-bcd5-429fd0d883af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344665} {:answer/id #uuid "a8330dbb-f5c9-5299-bc1b-9981f49ba19c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344666} {:answer/id #uuid "48a27676-4055-5228-989a-09d63ae11bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344667} {:answer/id #uuid "c1281efd-4609-5a12-af2e-c1dc46695e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa1d0c2a-f56e-5eae-bcd5-429fd0d883af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344665} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdb8acac-21e5-5c96-9cc4-89206a67215b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17762e70-5ac2-5a8b-a38d-88dab34b7f4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-337637" :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{x}+2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(5y+2x{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{x}+2{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=5y+2x{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int 2{x}^{-1}\\text{ }\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}[(\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{x}+2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(5y+2x{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{(5x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{(5{x}^{2}y+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=5x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{3},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=5{x}^{2}y+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (5x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (5{x}^{2}y+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two expressions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y=c.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "161b35e2-52bf-5f27-9fc3-bfe34eeb7935" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integrating Factor for an Inexact Equation: Condition on Y Satisfied"}] :question/a