dent :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3y}{x({y}^{3}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344698} {:answer/id #uuid "58a4353c-5663-5be0-82a4-e63b783d576f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{{y}^{3}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2649711e-44d7-503e-a3f8-b3ac32ddf6b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344696} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc6f4964-ef3d-589c-b4fb-d508a60f5014" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344695} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2da7175c-48e4-51a1-97c1-23ff2f7f662b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344701} {:answer/id #uuid "8bb0b233-7358-5a4e-b8c7-08ebc113fc17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-({y}^{3}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344702} {:answer/id #uuid "c99d4adc-7e5c-58f0-aa9c-349c029c3927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344703} {:answer/id #uuid "1aa54fb6-9ac8-5140-820a-045fe2769401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aa54fb6-9ac8-5140-820a-045fe2769401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344704} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a09abb0-f2a9-58e8-b6ba-4e39b3e8bf4e" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344700} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78d4a2b4-a769-50f6-87cb-5c57b02730db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}+1}(x({y}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344706} {:answer/id #uuid "a8bd1e5e-54ac-55d5-9720-4ff024d81fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-({y}^{3}+1)}(x({y}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344707} {:answer/id #uuid "f303cfc5-6fe0-5405-9804-6df585d38b5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3y}(x({y}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344708} {:answer/id #uuid "07f5b30c-8a7c-5fab-abb3-167bb92dc494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({y}^{3}+1)(x({y}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78d4a2b4-a769-50f6-87cb-5c57b02730db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}+1}(x({y}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344706} :answer-field/id #uuid "c18af9c9-8f1a-519e-b965-57839ed171bb" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b849a19-4e47-5a5d-8161-ea71201d5d93" :question/math-academy-id "q-337707" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}{y}^{′}=-x{y}^{3}-x,\\,x>0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form. This gives\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=x({y}^{3}+1),\\,Q(x,y)={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $y$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-7}},\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form: \n\n$$\nx({y}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nLet's denote \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=x({y}^{3}+1),\\,Q(x,y)={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x({y}^{3}+1))=3x{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{x({y}^{3}+1)}(3x{y}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int \\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{y}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{3}+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot (x({y}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y)=I(y)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}+1}(x({y}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a016197-61af-57f3-9c3a-05410db1374d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inexact Differential Equations Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51000411-4f8c-54d2-b4d9-57d430befa22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344712} {:answer/id #uuid "f84c38f2-228f-5d45-b011-25fe1962c53b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344713} {:answer/id #uuid "042e1d08-57bc-58f6-aa79-12e254d80575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344714} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8ebeca-18e1-5fd4-805c-d98949948da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "042e1d08-57bc-58f6-aa79-12e254d80575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344714} :answer-field/id #uuid "564ec897-066a-5164-b2d8-eeced4191b82" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344711} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dd94952-e1e2-568d-88fd-32835223cdaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+3y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344717} {:answer/id #uuid "be19ebe2-a9f4-519e-91ed-8b4bb0d4ee19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344718} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9386c3-0aa2-5007-97e8-b32791ee6284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+3y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344719} {:answer/id #uuid "f8aa28a3-00f2-5585-8238-358ffe717cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+3y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8aa28a3-00f2-5585-8238-358ffe717cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+3y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344720} :answer-field/id #uuid "5eeff95b-a1a4-58dd-b8fd-ae5f0adf3dc6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5b12f52-bca9-597a-88b5-c5036d3e9da3" :question/math-academy-id "q-338027" :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n(3x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{3}{x})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3x+{y}^{2},\\,Q(x,y)=xy+\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int {x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x[(3x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{3}{x})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{(3{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{({x}^{2}y+3)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)={x}^{2}y+3.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (3{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}y+3)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+3y+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=3y,\\,G(x)={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+3y=c.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a016197-61af-57f3-9c3a-05410db1374d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inexact Differential Equations Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b929de8-525c-5e9a-bd20-015d0e023860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344723} {:answer/id #uuid "9d841582-ef64-5e82-ae1c-1991ed409535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344724} {:answer/id #uuid "91a790a4-2480-51a7-bc83-bb53f53c799f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344725} {:answer/id #uuid "48e8cd5b-5cd2-5140-8ebe-be1cb68bcded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b929de8-525c-5e9a-bd20-015d0e023860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344723} :answer-field/id #uuid "040e8a5f-ac98-5813-aa9a-ff2de83c768a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344722} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4c71647-8286-54e6-af38-1646c8f297f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{3}}-5y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344728} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7eb397-ed9d-5636-aec1-9258edee7c56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}-5{y}^{2}=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344729} {:answer/id #uuid "54b97cbc-32c1-59e7-8e46-3f02ec94a87c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}-5y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344730} {:answer/id #uuid "28558548-a899-5cb2-b76b-8996387567ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}-5y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54b97cbc-32c1-59e7-8e46-3f02ec94a87c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}-5y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344730} :answer-field/id #uuid "1683638d-d7cd-5619-9daa-0f3c055d4a07" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c79cf20-94ce-52db-bcb7-fa0507cc280e" :question/math-academy-id "q-338070" :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\nxy\\,\\text{d}x-({x}^{2}+5{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=xy,\\,Q(x,y)=-{x}^{2}-5{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(y)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{y}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x,y) & ={e}^{-\\int 3/y\\text{ }\\text{d}y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}(yx\\,\\text{d}x-({x}^{2}+5{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ {{(\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{(-1)(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}+5)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}-5.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}-5)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}-5y+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=-5y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}-5y=c.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a016197-61af-57f3-9c3a-05410db1374d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inexact Differential Equations Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3666c2-e095-5d05-b84b-9ff50ea22f8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344734} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac29ab9-8b9f-5c22-a10e-a2074f3fe916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344735} {:answer/id #uuid "409e4621-4ada-5cbb-abbc-5f85ddedc203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344736} {:answer/id #uuid "609de797-93d4-5b26-9030-20d9ba575865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ac29ab9-8b9f-5c22-a10e-a2074f3fe916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344735} :answer-field/id #uuid "affff4cd-6379-5f9c-a0f1-9015455b71bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344733} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "846744a5-e246-5597-8c9e-4595bd7160e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}-y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344739} {:answer/id #uuid "af7a7a30-a935-58ff-90fa-9e209cc72a13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}-\\frac{y}{2}=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344740} {:answer/id #uuid "a530ab23-b0e8-5130-8871-4a3f6447b5b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344741} {:answer/id #uuid "acbf2103-f165-5e07-a3e4-28ebfceabdff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a530ab23-b0e8-5130-8871-4a3f6447b5b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344741} :answer-field/id #uuid "785fbd4b-2037-5362-a3e9-96bde34f5c34" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "39041e42-a797-5920-a1d7-b2d049613df0" :question/math-academy-id "q-338870" :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{y}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(y)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{y}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x,y) & ={e}^{-\\int 2/y\\text{ }\\text{d}y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}({x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ {{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{(-1)(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}+y)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}-y.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}-y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c.\n$$"}]}
